It was a commitment to the
Bible, but more so than anything,

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:19.050
it was, it was commitment to an ideology.

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:21.970
It was commitment to a
system of ideas that, yes,

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:24.040
have their roots in the Bible,

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:26.690
but me and you both came
from a reform background.

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:29.120
You, like, you came from like a specific,

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:30.627
like your church was specifically like,

00:12:30.627 --> 00:12:32.440
"Hey, we are reformed."

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:35.750
And my church was like, "we're
not a reformed denomination

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:37.500
but this particular expression of this

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:40.990
non-denominational denomination
is reformed," right?

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:43.090
- Yeah, post-college, newlywed,

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:44.920
the churches we were involved in, yes.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:47.070
- Right, so we basically,

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:48.940
even though I was uncomfortable with it,

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:53.090
I kind of subscribed to
sort of Calvinism light,

00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:54.790
you know, like what John Calvin taught.

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:57.440
So you've got the P in tulip,

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:59.040
which is per perseverance of the saints.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:00.880
This is a deep cut for those of you

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:03.042
who don't have any experience with this.

00:13:03.042 --> 00:13:04.533
- At least you skipped to the P.

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:07.810
You left the tuli on
the table, that's good.

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:11.350
- Essentially it's this once
saved always saved thing

00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:13.430
that, again there's a
biblical support for it.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:15.270
There's also some biblical,
there's some stuff

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:16.520
that might bring it into question,

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:19.420
but the general sort of
consensus of the churches

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:22.050
that we came from and the sort of the part

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:23.870
of the evangelical church
that we came from is that

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:25.550
once saved always saved.

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:28.900
And so I just find it
interesting that, you know,

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:32.800
you hear a couple of guys who
talk about their experience

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:35.550
with Christianity, how
they were all in for Jesus.

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:37.990
They recognized that they were sinners

00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:40.560
in desperate need of the grace of God

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:41.720
that comes only through accepting

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:44.480
the sacrifice of Jesus on our behalf.

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:47.380
And we made a decision, in
my case multiple decisions

00:13:47.380 --> 00:13:51.640
just to make sure it'd take,
it had taken to accept Christ.

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:55.510
And you say "those guys
were never Christians"

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:58.343
because that is what you have to believe.

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:01.763
The power of ideology.

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:08.230
But the thing that I
really want to get into

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:11.320
is the timing of us telling the stories.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:13.590
- So before you move on, so I mean,

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:15.143
you're obviously asserting,

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:18.960
and I will, I'll agree
from my perspective too,

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:23.960
that experience that yeah,
I believed I was saved.

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:27.900
I mean, period.

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:29.680
I don't know what else to say.

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:34.680
I don't look back on that
experience and, you know,

00:14:34.979 --> 00:14:39.920
I'm very critical of myself, but I don't,

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:43.750
I just don't think that, yeah,

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:46.633
I don't, I don't look back
at my former self that way.

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:50.093
And that's what you're saying too?

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:53.880
You think you were as
real as you could be?

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:56.390
I mean, did we miss something?

00:14:56.390 --> 00:14:59.990
- What I would say to committed
evangelical Christians

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:03.390
who are listening is
the way that they feel

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:04.817
about their relationship with God

00:15:04.817 --> 00:15:08.420
and the way that they experience
their relationship with God

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:11.790
is I would say, as well as I
can without being in your own,

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:15.090
without being inside your
body, I would say, I get it,

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:18.163
I relate, I understand, I
had the same experience.

00:15:20.355 --> 00:15:22.480
And now I think very
differently about that,

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:24.270
and we would have
fundamental differences as to

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:27.500
what we think was actually
going on at that time.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:31.940
But I'm saying that I understand
that if you subscribe to,

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:33.750
if you're still in and
you subscribe to this

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:36.860
particular ideology,
you do not have a choice

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:38.500
but to believe that we
were never Christians

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:41.423
or that we still are, and
we will return someday.

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:44.200
That's all, that's all I'm saying.

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:46.470
So I'm not, I'm just
saying it's interesting

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:47.730
that that's where people landed,

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:51.270
and the power of you can actually,

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:54.390
the way that has been discussed
in a couple of places,

00:15:54.390 --> 00:15:57.102
the way that we have been
described as like, you know,

00:15:57.102 --> 00:15:59.540
you can kind of tell
when you listen to them

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:03.280
that they never really
came to a saving faith.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:04.850
They never really repented.

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:06.550
They never really understood grace.

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:08.420
It seems like it was cultural.

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:10.083
It seems like it was,

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:13.010
maybe there was an
intellectual commitment,

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:14.100
but it doesn't seem like they had

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:16.604
given their hearts to Jesus.

00:16:16.604 --> 00:16:19.490
And as somebody who made
those same arguments

00:16:19.490 --> 00:16:22.960
to lots of people, who talked about

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:24.750
it's not about a religion,
it's a relationship.

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:27.780
It's about a group. It's the gift.

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:32.417
It is the undeserved gift
of God, grace through faith.

00:16:32.417 --> 00:16:34.120
That's the only way.

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:35.520
That's what I believed.
That's what I lived.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:36.820
That's what I experienced.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:41.603
And to be told, no, you didn't,

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:45.090
I'm not mad at those
people for saying that.

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:47.830
I'm saying I understand
that when you have,

00:16:47.830 --> 00:16:49.840
when you've completely
bought into the ideology

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:51.810
you have no choice but to believe that.

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:52.923
Just an observation.

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:56.010
But the timing, the timing of our story,

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:58.820
what are the chances that
we told these stories

00:16:58.820 --> 00:17:03.783
at the onset of 2020, just
before the world fell apart,

00:17:04.937 --> 00:17:09.485
and listen, I'm very grateful
that we were able to do this

00:17:09.485 --> 00:17:12.280
at the beginning of
what has ended up being

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:14.450
basically the most
difficult world for the,

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:16.320
or difficult year for the world at large

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:17.620
in our lifetimes, right?

00:17:17.620 --> 00:17:18.540
- Oh yeah.

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:19.770
- Some people have suffered a lot more.

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:24.770
Me and you have had the privilege
of being in the position

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:26.830
that we are and having
the jobs that we are

00:17:26.830 --> 00:17:28.420
so that we were able to
get through this year

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:29.893
relatively unscathed.

00:17:30.995 --> 00:17:32.770
A lot of people didn't
and the world didn't

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:34.420
and the world is still suffering.

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:38.130
Two, two things about that.

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:38.963
First thing is

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:46.120
having been open and vulnerable
about our spiritual journey

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:52.540
gave us an opportunity to
then speak open and vulnerably

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:55.963
about the things that happened in 2020.

00:17:55.963 --> 00:17:56.796
- Yeah.

00:17:56.796 --> 00:17:58.620
- And you know, two guys who
have been in the public eye

00:17:58.620 --> 00:17:59.580
for a really long time,

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:04.180
who had kept this most
significant thing secret

00:18:05.794 --> 00:18:09.600
and off limits, we had
kind of become experts

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:11.460
at navigating interviews

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:15.840
without talking about
the elephant in the room,

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:17.480
which is like, "but weren't
you guys like Christians?

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:18.313
And not, not just Christians,

00:18:18.313 --> 00:18:19.597
but like big Christians,
like professional Christians?

00:18:19.597 --> 00:18:21.910
Didn't you guys write
like some Christian songs

00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:24.620
for kids that were on like DVDs that were

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:26.350
in constant rotation at
Christian bookstores?"

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:27.763
Yes, yes, yes, and yes,

00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:30.040
but not talking about that,

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:31.810
it was this weird thing
that as we continue

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:34.410
to get more vulnerable on this podcast

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:36.313
and just in life in general,

00:18:39.870 --> 00:18:44.870
continuing to try to balance
that, not sharing that thing.

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:47.580
And then what became a
year where there were

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:50.990
certain things that our
nation and our world faced,

00:18:50.990 --> 00:18:54.100
where the expectation was
if you are a public figure

00:18:54.100 --> 00:18:56.770
with an audience at all,

00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:58.680
you're going to speak into these things.

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:00.550
Like you can't really get away.

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:02.180
In 2020, you couldn't get away with

00:19:02.180 --> 00:19:04.550
just staying on the sidelines
about a lot of things

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:06.330
that we went through as a nation,

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:09.362
but boy, it made it a lot easier

00:19:09.362 --> 00:19:14.160
to engage in that conversation
having gotten vulnerable.

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:15.690
I don't, it would have
been weird to not have

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:17.500
had that conversation at
the beginning of 2020.

00:19:17.500 --> 00:19:20.300
I honestly can't imagine
our interaction publicly

00:19:20.300 --> 00:19:24.123
with the year that was 2020
without having told our stories.

00:19:25.990 --> 00:19:29.830
- You know, I'm surprised that we're,

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:32.710
just as a, this is a
side note, that we were,

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:35.740
that we've been as
successful as we've been

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:38.570
and gotten to the point
in our careers we have

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:41.560
without being vulnerable to that level,

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:44.947
because that's kind of a big part of

00:19:44.947 --> 00:19:47.680
being an internet personality.

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:50.210
- We wrote a whole book that was about us.

00:19:50.210 --> 00:19:51.200
I mean, let's be honest.

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:53.443
We wrote the "Book of Mythicality"

00:19:53.443 --> 00:19:54.830
that was full of stories about us.

00:19:54.830 --> 00:19:57.200
It was memoir-ish,

00:19:57.200 --> 00:19:59.683
but yet it didn't talk
about any of this stuff.

00:20:01.418 --> 00:20:02.310
Because we weren't ready.

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:05.130
- And I do think there
was feedback like, okay,

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:07.170
they have more to gain
by sharing this story

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:09.750
than keeping it to themselves.

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:11.910
And you know, the people
that are suffering

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:14.010
are the ones who are, their faith is,

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:16.440
you know, is not strong enough yet.

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:20.530
So they're, they're
capitalizing on a vulnerability

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:25.530
for their own, to boost
their popularity even further

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:30.670
at the expense of people who
are floundering spiritually.

00:20:30.670 --> 00:20:31.930
And it's, you know.

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:34.296
- I'll talk a little bit
about that in a little bit.

00:20:34.296 --> 00:20:38.440
- Okay, we'll come back to that, but okay.

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:40.988
I'll just wait then if
you're gonna talk about it.

00:20:40.988 --> 00:20:43.320
- But the, but the,
the bigger significance

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:45.890
- I'm grateful that, to your point,

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:49.530
back on point, I'm grateful
that the timing worked out

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:51.360
the way it did, that we were
able to talk about that.

00:20:51.360 --> 00:20:53.425
And then it led to the conversation

00:20:53.425 --> 00:20:56.575
in the episode we had about Ben, I mean.

00:20:56.575 --> 00:20:59.190
- We couldn't have had
that, I mean, again.

00:20:59.190 --> 00:21:00.620
- I just felt like it was so important.

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:02.380
- The biggest episode of "Ear Biscuits,"

00:21:02.380 --> 00:21:04.500
and that's not what this
is about, but like, yeah.

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:05.333
- We had no clue.

00:21:05.333 --> 00:21:06.890
- Had no clue that people
were gonna watch it

00:21:06.890 --> 00:21:07.723
to the degree they did.

00:21:07.723 --> 00:21:12.000
- But it was important
for us to process that

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:16.140
and to make it, for people
to take it how they would,

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:21.140
for us to process that aspect of our lives

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:23.130
and how our spiritual
journey had an impact on

00:21:23.130 --> 00:21:25.280
one of the most important relationships

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:27.030
that we had growing up
with our other best friend.

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:29.463
- And we can talk all
about Ben and Ben's death,

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:33.130
but to talk about Ben's
death without talking about

00:21:33.130 --> 00:21:36.040
what we were trying to do during his,

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:36.980
with him on his deathbed

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:39.857
and trying to bring him
to a decision for Jesus,

00:21:39.857 --> 00:21:41.730
and the way that we processed that now

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:43.663
would have been a totally different story.

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:47.670
- And, yeah, to be,

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:52.310
to be in this medium and not
be able to be true to yourself

00:21:52.310 --> 00:21:56.020
will eat away at your soul.

00:21:56.020 --> 00:21:56.853
- Yeah.

00:21:56.853 --> 00:21:58.830
- And that's, that's
why I'm grateful for it.

00:21:58.830 --> 00:22:03.830
- Yeah, I mean, it feels, I don't know.

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:05.820
I would have exploded.

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:06.653
- Yeah.

00:22:06.653 --> 00:22:07.486
- I would have exploded this year.

00:22:07.486 --> 00:22:08.820
- I, I feel emotional about it

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:10.840
just because I'm, I'm grateful.

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:15.360
I mean, it's, I got a lot to
process in the next episode.

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:19.270
I'm not gonna, but I'm grateful for it.

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:21.790
And yeah, everything
that we've gone through

00:22:21.790 --> 00:22:24.832
as a world and as a
nation that I, you know,

00:22:24.832 --> 00:22:29.832
I'm grateful that we were able
to bring more of ourselves

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:34.715
to that than before the lost years series,

00:22:34.715 --> 00:22:38.660
we were, I felt like we
were ready or capable

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:42.400
or had the, had the confidence to do.

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:43.233
- Yeah. Right.

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:49.040
But I think that the
bigger significance for me

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:54.040
was the way that my story,
in us telling our stories,

00:22:54.880 --> 00:22:56.860
intersected with the evangelical church's

00:22:56.860 --> 00:22:58.690
response to 2020 in general.

00:22:58.690 --> 00:23:00.030
- Hmm. Okay.

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:03.100
- And I am going spend
a fair amount of time

00:23:03.100 --> 00:23:05.980
talking about that today, but first.

00:23:05.980 --> 00:23:06.813
- Oh gosh.

00:23:06.813 --> 00:23:10.083
- Let's sell a feel good
Mythical Morning hat.

00:23:13.240 --> 00:23:14.320
- This is a cool hat.

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:16.230
I mean, if I put it on,
I'm just gonna look stupid

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:17.960
'cause I don't want to mess up my hair.

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:18.793
- Oh, is that what it is?

00:23:18.793 --> 00:23:20.290
- That's what matters most.
- Okay.

00:23:20.290 --> 00:23:22.720
- Let's not forget that.

00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:24.920
Feel Good Mythical
Morning, it comes on a hat.

00:23:24.920 --> 00:23:26.450
It comes on a hoodie. It comes on a shirt.

00:23:26.450 --> 00:23:28.050
It comes on all types of things.

00:23:28.914 --> 00:23:30.230
- I think that's called a six panel.

00:23:30.230 --> 00:23:33.270
- This right here, my friend is,

00:23:33.270 --> 00:23:35.550
I'm super proud of what this represents.

00:23:35.550 --> 00:23:36.383
- Feel good, man.

00:23:36.383 --> 00:23:38.030
- That if you get, you watch
"Good Mythical Morning."

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:39.080
- If it feels good, do it.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:40.370
- I don't care.
- That what you're saying?

00:23:40.370 --> 00:23:41.203
