00:52:15.960 --> 00:52:19.050
She has a wife, they have two kids.

00:52:19.050 --> 00:52:24.000
We went to Legoland and we
happened to run into them.

00:52:24.000 --> 00:52:25.723
This is before the pandemic hit.

00:52:26.820 --> 00:52:31.820
And it was, you know, I
got a kick out of Lando

00:52:33.090 --> 00:52:36.460
seeing his teacher, who he loves so much,

00:52:36.460 --> 00:52:38.700
in a different environment, you know?

00:52:38.700 --> 00:52:42.850
You know, it's that, there's
always that funny exchange

00:52:42.850 --> 00:52:46.200
between a student and their
teacher out in the wild,

00:52:46.200 --> 00:52:51.200
and it feels weird, but his
love for her was so strong

00:52:52.500 --> 00:52:56.340
that it had the power to
overcome that weirdness.

00:52:56.340 --> 00:52:58.940
And like, they, we got a picture together

00:52:58.940 --> 00:53:00.850
and we would see each other in line.

00:53:00.850 --> 00:53:03.200
We talked to each other
and like, you know,

00:53:03.200 --> 00:53:06.470
it was amazing just that he has

00:53:06.470 --> 00:53:10.890
that type of relationship
with his teacher.

00:53:10.890 --> 00:53:15.890
If I would have instilled in
him what was instilled in me,

00:53:17.030 --> 00:53:20.110
he would have not,

00:53:20.110 --> 00:53:24.520
he would have felt deep
down within himself

00:53:24.520 --> 00:53:27.083
that he didn't have
permission to love her.

00:53:28.500 --> 00:53:31.403
That was my experience, you know, because,

00:53:32.254 --> 00:53:34.940
and I know that that's, you know,

00:53:34.940 --> 00:53:37.970
you can pick that statement
apart easily because,

00:53:37.970 --> 00:53:39.873
well there's a difference between love--

00:53:39.873 --> 00:53:41.280
- Love the sinner, hate the sin.

00:53:41.280 --> 00:53:44.850
- Yeah, but I know what
the experience is like

00:53:44.850 --> 00:53:48.880
and I know how I looked
at people who were,

00:53:48.880 --> 00:53:53.260
who weren't straight, but the thing is,

00:53:53.260 --> 00:53:57.743
and to your point, Lando knows,

00:54:00.480 --> 00:54:03.450
he knows what it's like to love somebody.

00:54:03.450 --> 00:54:04.540
That's not something that--

00:54:04.540 --> 00:54:05.373
- It's inherent.

00:54:05.373 --> 00:54:07.920
- And it's not something
that I had to teach him.

00:54:07.920 --> 00:54:12.640
And the fact that he's free to love her

00:54:13.790 --> 00:54:16.917
is a beautiful thing that I--

00:54:16.917 --> 00:54:18.270
- And to love her fully.

00:54:18.270 --> 00:54:20.230
- To love her fully, yes.

00:54:20.230 --> 00:54:21.390
- When you love, 'cause again,

00:54:21.390 --> 00:54:22.960
I'm not saying that Christians who,

00:54:22.960 --> 00:54:24.600
there are Christians who believe,

00:54:24.600 --> 00:54:26.930
based on their commitment to the Bible

00:54:26.930 --> 00:54:28.520
and to their interpretation of the Bible,

00:54:28.520 --> 00:54:30.900
that being gay is ultimately wrong,

00:54:30.900 --> 00:54:35.900
but they say that, or gay acts are wrong,

00:54:35.900 --> 00:54:39.660
but the person is lovable and
can be redeemed, et cetera.

00:54:39.660 --> 00:54:40.493
But the thing that that mentality--

00:54:40.493 --> 00:54:44.241
- It just doesn't work out
that way in your heart.

00:54:44.241 --> 00:54:47.720
- You're invalidating a huge
part of that person's identity

00:54:47.720 --> 00:54:51.090
and existence, and also,
in your teacher's case,

00:54:51.090 --> 00:54:53.579
the relationship and her family

00:54:53.579 --> 00:54:56.710
is not a valid or legitimate family.

00:54:56.710 --> 00:54:59.050
And therefore that's not
full, it's just not full love.

00:54:59.050 --> 00:55:01.200
- And he, and I didn't
have to teach him that.

00:55:01.200 --> 00:55:02.560
- Yeah. Right.

00:55:02.560 --> 00:55:05.590
- He knows, he knows what full,

00:55:05.590 --> 00:55:09.090
wholehearted, true love for somebody is.

00:55:09.090 --> 00:55:12.790
And I'll leave it at that.

00:55:12.790 --> 00:55:14.830
- So if you want your kids
to stay in the church,

00:55:14.830 --> 00:55:16.440
again this is unsolicited advice.

00:55:16.440 --> 00:55:19.400
I don't know if anybody's
taking it, but this is not hey,

00:55:19.400 --> 00:55:21.630
I want all your kids to leave the church.

00:55:21.630 --> 00:55:23.710
That's not what I'm saying at all.

00:55:23.710 --> 00:55:25.730
If you want your kids
to stay in the church,

00:55:25.730 --> 00:55:27.460
you don't need to change your kids.

00:55:27.460 --> 00:55:29.010
You need to change your church.

00:55:31.390 --> 00:55:33.623
If your kids can't find Jesus,

00:55:34.710 --> 00:55:37.317
the Jesus that they know from the Bible,

00:55:37.317 --> 00:55:39.660
the Jesus that you've taught them about.

00:55:39.660 --> 00:55:43.160
If they can't find him within
the walls of your church,

00:55:43.160 --> 00:55:45.650
you know what, they will go
looking for him elsewhere.

00:55:45.650 --> 00:55:48.480
And that may not be what
they say they're doing.

00:55:48.480 --> 00:55:49.900
That may not be how they articulate it.

00:55:49.900 --> 00:55:51.300
It may not be how they describe it,

00:55:51.300 --> 00:55:54.190
but I believe that
that's what's happening.

00:55:54.190 --> 00:55:58.515
And it's interesting because, you know,

00:55:58.515 --> 00:56:01.417
people made assumptions
about, about me and you,

00:56:01.417 --> 00:56:03.370
and they're making the same
assumptions about their kids.

00:56:03.370 --> 00:56:04.203
Let me explain.

00:56:04.203 --> 00:56:06.400
So a lot of people assume
that we left the church

00:56:06.400 --> 00:56:08.210
because there was something out there,

00:56:08.210 --> 00:56:09.300
something outside of the church,

00:56:09.300 --> 00:56:12.300
whether that'd be fame,
fortune, cultural acceptance,

00:56:12.300 --> 00:56:15.880
whatever that we ultimately
wanted more than Jesus, right?

00:56:15.880 --> 00:56:17.980
Like we fell to the temptation

00:56:17.980 --> 00:56:20.690
and we went after that
thing instead of Jesus.

00:56:20.690 --> 00:56:24.080
And you also think that your
kids might want something

00:56:24.080 --> 00:56:26.070
out there, something outside of the church

00:56:26.070 --> 00:56:29.020
more than they want Jesus.

00:56:29.020 --> 00:56:30.360
I think it might be simpler than that.

00:56:30.360 --> 00:56:33.190
I think maybe your kids
who left the church

00:56:33.190 --> 00:56:34.610
just wanted Jesus

00:56:34.610 --> 00:56:36.883
and they saw that you
wanted something else.

00:56:39.370 --> 00:56:40.850
They see that the church at large

00:56:40.850 --> 00:56:42.663
seems to want something else.

00:56:46.030 --> 00:56:50.500
Because in my experience,
and just looking at it,

00:56:50.500 --> 00:56:53.120
and again, regardless of your conclusions

00:56:53.120 --> 00:56:55.610
about the nature of the
historicity of Jesus

00:56:55.610 --> 00:56:57.160
and the specific words
that are in the Bible,

00:56:57.160 --> 00:56:59.600
but if you just take the words of Jesus,

00:56:59.600 --> 00:57:02.543
the life of Jesus, the teachings
of Jesus at face value,

00:57:05.370 --> 00:57:09.060
you have to do some very athletic,

00:57:09.060 --> 00:57:13.040
very athletic mental gymnastics

00:57:13.040 --> 00:57:16.650
to find a way to reconcile
the way the white evangelical

00:57:16.650 --> 00:57:20.163
church looks in America today
with any of those words.

00:57:22.550 --> 00:57:25.030
I just can't put those
puzzle pieces together

00:57:25.030 --> 00:57:26.333
in a way that makes sense.

00:57:29.700 --> 00:57:34.700
Okay, I stepped into a
giant pile of bull crap

00:57:35.593 --> 00:57:38.390
and we'll see how that
airs out on the internet,

00:57:38.390 --> 00:57:40.280
but let's move on. (laughs)

00:57:40.280 --> 00:57:41.113
- Okay.

00:57:42.624 --> 00:57:44.910
- In an effort to kind of finish this up

00:57:46.528 --> 00:57:48.653
with where I'm at spiritually, right?

00:57:51.110 --> 00:57:52.780
So I've covered some of this ground,

00:57:52.780 --> 00:57:55.633
but just by way of just kind
of putting it all together.

00:57:57.090 --> 00:57:58.740
You know, when I was a Christian,

00:57:59.647 --> 00:58:02.290
I was obsessed with
having it all figured out,

00:58:02.290 --> 00:58:04.380
like understanding my faith,

00:58:04.380 --> 00:58:07.000
rederiving my faith over and over again,

00:58:07.000 --> 00:58:08.733
it was a relationship with God.

00:58:09.890 --> 00:58:13.280
But the way that I fed that
relationship was with knowledge,

00:58:13.280 --> 00:58:15.000
knowing scripture, knowing more truth,

00:58:15.000 --> 00:58:16.660
teaching, that kind of thing,

00:58:16.660 --> 00:58:18.940
especially in the traditions
that we came in, came from,

00:58:18.940 --> 00:58:23.850
you know, it's a very
mind-based tradition,

00:58:23.850 --> 00:58:25.850
sort of the more reformed you get

00:58:25.850 --> 00:58:27.440
in the white evangelical church,

00:58:27.440 --> 00:58:30.950
the sort of mind-based
intellectual it gets.

00:58:30.950 --> 00:58:32.080
And the more you kind of move into

00:58:32.080 --> 00:58:34.690
more of a charismatic
Pentecostal experience,

00:58:34.690 --> 00:58:36.150
it gets a little bit more heart-based.

00:58:36.150 --> 00:58:38.340
And I never really had that.

00:58:38.340 --> 00:58:40.100
I went to those kinds
of churches at times,

00:58:40.100 --> 00:58:42.700
but didn't have, that wasn't
my personal experience.

00:58:45.500 --> 00:58:50.500
But today, I see spirituality

00:58:50.570 --> 00:58:53.110
as an openness to things that transcend

00:58:53.110 --> 00:58:54.730
my conceptual understanding,

00:58:54.730 --> 00:58:57.323
but are still deeply meaningful, right?

00:58:59.310 --> 00:59:03.960
And I think that most people,

00:59:03.960 --> 00:59:05.677
now some people don't,
some people are like

00:59:05.677 --> 00:59:08.230
"I don't need, I don't need
faith. I don't need religion.

00:59:08.230 --> 00:59:10.893
I don't need spirituality,"
full, full stop.

00:59:12.388 --> 00:59:16.670
I think that the majority
of people do need something

00:59:16.670 --> 00:59:18.743
that they might describe as spirituality.

00:59:20.130 --> 00:59:23.720
And you know, religion sort
of represents a human effort

00:59:23.720 --> 00:59:25.860
to systematize those
glimpses of connection

00:59:25.860 --> 00:59:27.100
that we have with something deeper,

00:59:27.100 --> 00:59:28.983
that leaning into the mystery.

00:59:31.505 --> 00:59:34.850
And one of the reasons that
it's so effective at it

00:59:34.850 --> 00:59:37.410
is that it happens in community, right?

00:59:37.410 --> 00:59:40.360
Religions, religion happens in community.

00:59:40.360 --> 00:59:43.153
It is like community and culture together

00:59:43.153 --> 00:59:45.360
kind of meets faith, and the places where

00:59:45.360 --> 00:59:46.660
it's more embedded in the culture

00:59:46.660 --> 00:59:47.547
and more embedded in the community

00:59:47.547 --> 00:59:49.550
are the places where it is the strongest

00:59:49.550 --> 00:59:51.893
and like last the longest, right?

00:59:53.010 --> 00:59:54.940
And there's this really
interesting thing that's happening

00:59:54.940 --> 00:59:56.900
all around the world, but
it's happening in our country

00:59:56.900 --> 01:00:00.360
as well as that, you know,
more people are moving into

01:00:00.360 --> 01:00:02.137
a new religious category
every single year,

01:00:02.137 --> 01:00:04.690
and that is the
religiously non-affiliated.

01:00:04.690 --> 01:00:06.560
And there really isn't
a community for that

01:00:06.560 --> 01:00:09.810
and I think that my prediction is that

01:00:11.030 --> 01:00:13.510
those chickens are gonna
come home to roost.

01:00:13.510 --> 01:00:17.060
And this idea that everybody's
gonna be disconnected

01:00:17.060 --> 01:00:20.400
and just religiously
unaffiliated is not as,

01:00:20.400 --> 01:00:22.370
I don't think it's a
sustainable way to move forward.

01:00:22.370 --> 01:00:25.440
And it may take a generation
or two, I don't know,

01:00:25.440 --> 01:00:28.480
before things come back around.

01:00:28.480 --> 01:00:31.170
And I don't have any
predictions as to what that is,

01:00:31.170 --> 01:00:34.493
but I kind of feel myself
in that group right now,

01:00:35.600 --> 01:00:37.324
but as it applies to me personally.

01:00:37.324 --> 01:00:38.220
- You're going to start a church.

01:00:38.220 --> 01:00:39.140
- No, no, I'm not.

01:00:39.140 --> 01:00:41.240
I'm very much not going to start a church.

01:00:43.170 --> 01:00:45.500
But as the way that I kind of
think about Christianity now,

01:00:45.500 --> 01:00:47.530
and again, this is all like,

01:00:47.530 --> 01:00:50.120
I'm just kind of opening up
my mind and heart a little bit

01:00:50.120 --> 01:00:51.410
and just saying that this, these are some,

01:00:51.410 --> 01:00:54.160
this is some of the way
that I think about it today.

01:00:54.160 --> 01:00:56.300
- But are you saying, just
a point of clarification,

01:00:56.300 --> 01:00:57.400
that you think that the,

01:00:57.400 --> 01:01:00.030
when you say it's gonna come
back around or to roost,

01:01:00.030 --> 01:01:02.150
you're talking about
the church will change

01:01:02.150 --> 01:01:04.780
to a point where then people
will be willing to go back in?

01:01:04.780 --> 01:01:08.090
- That is one of several options.

01:01:08.090 --> 01:01:09.590
That's one of several options.

01:01:11.530 --> 01:01:14.130
I'm just saying that I
don't think that religious

01:01:14.130 --> 01:01:17.540
unaffiliation is a sustainable condition

01:01:17.540 --> 01:01:19.080
for the average person.

01:01:19.080 --> 01:01:19.913
- Hmm.

01:01:20.795 --> 01:01:24.497
- And, and I'm not,
again, that doesn't mean

01:01:24.497 --> 01:01:27.710
that it means a return to
traditional understanding

01:01:27.710 --> 01:01:28.810
or traditional religion.

01:01:28.810 --> 01:01:32.130
- It might just mean a resurgence in 4-H.

01:01:32.130 --> 01:01:33.760
- I'm just saying that if you,

01:01:33.760 --> 01:01:37.630
and if you look at the
human makeup, right,

01:01:37.630 --> 01:01:40.350
if you just look at the
composition of a person

01:01:40.350 --> 01:01:42.810
and you had a way to break them down,

01:01:42.810 --> 01:01:45.280
you would find something within them,

01:01:45.280 --> 01:01:47.770
or just it's something that you
would interpret in the whole

01:01:47.770 --> 01:01:52.350
that is a need for a connection

01:01:52.350 --> 01:01:53.680
with something beyond themselves.

01:01:53.680 --> 01:01:54.550
- A social bond, a social component.

01:01:54.550 --> 01:01:55.820
- Well.

01:01:55.820 --> 01:01:56.690
- A relational component.

01:01:56.690 --> 01:01:59.710
- It might be that it's
strictly biological

01:01:59.710 --> 01:02:02.870
and it can be satisfied
with human connection alone,

01:02:02.870 --> 01:02:05.660
but I just don't, for
me I think that it feels

01:02:05.660 --> 01:02:08.700
deeper and bigger than that, and so--

01:02:08.700 --> 01:02:11.810
- But you're saying that you personally

01:02:11.810 --> 01:02:15.440
are missing and some experience.

01:02:15.440 --> 01:02:17.813
- No, no, well, I'm not missing it.

01:02:19.210 --> 01:02:20.043
I'll get into that.

01:02:20.043 --> 01:02:20.876
- Okay.

01:02:20.876 --> 01:02:23.130
- So okay, so I think a
lot of people in the West

01:02:23.130 --> 01:02:25.890
including me, you know,
we think about religion

01:02:25.890 --> 01:02:28.290
in a deeply personal way,
meaning that we think about it

01:02:28.290 --> 01:02:29.760
in terms of our individual experience

01:02:29.760 --> 01:02:32.363
or individual destiny,
our individual soul,

01:02:33.402 --> 01:02:37.923
and Christianity is very
individualistic, right?

01:02:38.881 --> 01:02:41.240
And there's a lot of reasons for this.

01:02:41.240 --> 01:02:43.070
Some of it has to do with
how much Greek philosophy

01:02:43.070 --> 01:02:44.623
influenced the writing of Paul,

01:02:45.568 --> 01:02:48.390
but it's been reinforced
over thousands of years

01:02:48.390 --> 01:02:51.090
as the West has become even
more and more individualistic.

01:02:51.090 --> 01:02:52.410
I mean, there's the, you
know the printing press

01:02:52.410 --> 01:02:53.860
where everybody had their own
Bible and that kind of thing

01:02:53.860 --> 01:02:56.520
and it kind of, the authority
kind of shifted away

01:02:56.520 --> 01:02:59.156
from just a few people reading.

01:02:59.156 --> 01:03:02.680
But this personal relationship
that we talk about

01:03:02.680 --> 01:03:04.250
in Christianity, it's not
something that you find

01:03:04.250 --> 01:03:05.350
much in Eastern religion.

01:03:05.350 --> 01:03:07.630
It is kind of a Western phenomenon, right?

01:03:07.630 --> 01:03:09.610
There's a much more of a
collective sense of identity

01:03:09.610 --> 01:03:11.240
and also religious identity in the East

01:03:11.240 --> 01:03:12.540
than there is in the West,

01:03:14.530 --> 01:03:17.600
but there's this other aspect
of the way Greek thought

01:03:17.600 --> 01:03:18.840
has influenced Christianity.

01:03:18.840 --> 01:03:21.730
And that is this distinction
between body and soul, right?

01:03:21.730 --> 01:03:24.280
And this distinction between
God and his creation,

01:03:24.280 --> 01:03:27.380
and there tends to be this
idea of separateness from God

01:03:27.380 --> 01:03:30.083
and separateness of soul and body.

01:03:30.083 --> 01:03:32.210
And then I think that that leads to,

01:03:32.210 --> 01:03:34.380
it continues this dualistic
thinking leads down this road

01:03:34.380 --> 01:03:36.206
where there's a distinction between

01:03:36.206 --> 01:03:37.217
the church and the world.

01:03:37.217 --> 01:03:40.684
And this is just an
observation about the church.

01:03:40.684 --> 01:03:43.350
And if you don't know, if
you're not in the church,

01:03:43.350 --> 01:03:45.180
you may not know that this is what most,

01:03:45.180 --> 01:03:46.603
all evangelicals believe.

01:03:48.299 --> 01:03:51.030
You know, and this is
what I believed, which is

01:03:51.030 --> 01:03:53.690
the church and the people, the
Christians within the church

01:03:53.690 --> 01:03:57.910
literally have the spirit
of God inside of them.

01:03:57.910 --> 01:04:00.160
And everyone who isn't
a believer is ultimately

01:04:00.160 --> 01:04:01.593
left to their own devices.

01:04:06.460 --> 01:04:09.700
That's another thing that
makes it hard to validate

01:04:09.700 --> 01:04:11.083
someone else's experience.

01:04:12.930 --> 01:04:15.870
When I believed that I literally had,

01:04:15.870 --> 01:04:17.350
so there's three parts of God, you know,

01:04:17.350 --> 01:04:18.660
the father, son, and the Holy spirit,

01:04:18.660 --> 01:04:20.900
and when you make that
decision to become a Christian,

01:04:20.900 --> 01:04:23.090
the Holy spirit in dwells, you,

01:04:23.090 --> 01:04:26.880
you literally have the spirit
of the almighty God within you

01:04:28.700 --> 01:04:30.870
leading you, guiding you.

01:04:30.870 --> 01:04:33.510
And when you're not a
Christian, you don't have that

01:04:33.510 --> 01:04:36.760
and so you're just a human
left to a human's devices

01:04:36.760 --> 01:04:38.750
and human's way of thinking.

01:04:38.750 --> 01:04:40.330
It's one of the reasons that Christians

01:04:40.330 --> 01:04:42.600
tend not to trust people
who aren't Christians.

01:04:42.600 --> 01:04:43.770
It's one of the reasons that Christians

01:04:43.770 --> 01:04:46.980
tend not to trust
authorities, like, you know,

01:04:46.980 --> 01:04:50.478
the scientists and what's
the government up to now?

01:04:50.478 --> 01:04:53.340
- Or a plumber without an ichthus fish

01:04:53.340 --> 01:04:54.850
on the back of his truck.

01:04:54.850 --> 01:04:55.683
- Yeah.

01:04:55.683 --> 01:04:57.290
- I mean, well, it is a secret code

01:04:57.290 --> 01:05:00.100
that I think does go that deep.

01:05:00.100 --> 01:05:01.532
- Well how can it not?

01:05:01.532 --> 01:05:02.500
- There's God, Holy spirit's in you,

01:05:02.500 --> 01:05:03.660
Holy spirit's in me, okay.

01:05:03.660 --> 01:05:06.450
- If I think that I've got
the spirit of God in me

01:05:06.450 --> 01:05:10.370
and I think that you don't, how?

01:05:10.370 --> 01:05:13.640
That we're in, we're kind
of in a different place.

01:05:13.640 --> 01:05:15.685
- Oh yeah.
- Right?

01:05:15.685 --> 01:05:17.600
That's consequential,

01:05:17.600 --> 01:05:19.230
I think that's consequential for humanity,

01:05:19.230 --> 01:05:21.370
and I think it's one of the reasons that,

01:05:21.370 --> 01:05:23.937
I think it's one of the
reasons that you can interpret,

01:05:23.937 --> 01:05:26.390
and I do interpret a lot of
the actions of Christians

01:05:26.390 --> 01:05:28.990
in 2020 as supremely selfish, right?

01:05:28.990 --> 01:05:32.420
You know, again, regardless of
what you think about a mask,

01:05:32.420 --> 01:05:35.030
again, the whole principle
of the mask is that

01:05:35.030 --> 01:05:37.470
it's not full-proof, but
that it, to some degree,

01:05:37.470 --> 01:05:39.280
depending on the nature of the mask,

01:05:39.280 --> 01:05:42.680
which is important, it
slows the spread of COVID.

01:05:42.680 --> 01:05:43.650
But what does it do?

01:05:43.650 --> 01:05:45.930
It mostly prevents the spread

01:05:45.930 --> 01:05:47.970
from the person wearing it, right?

01:05:47.970 --> 01:05:51.997
So it is a supremely
selfless thing to wear it.

01:05:51.997 --> 01:05:56.261
And it is a pretty selfish
thing not to wear it, right?

01:05:56.261 --> 01:05:58.380
When it comes to the vaccine, again,

01:05:58.380 --> 01:06:00.290
the whole idea of vaccination

01:06:02.180 --> 01:06:06.900
is that the collective gets
vaccinated to a certain rate

01:06:06.900 --> 01:06:10.050
in order, you know,
individuals get vaccinated

01:06:10.050 --> 01:06:13.370
at a certain rate to
protect the collective.

01:06:13.370 --> 01:06:14.437
And for every person who says

01:06:14.437 --> 01:06:15.660
"well, I'm gonna see what happens.

01:06:15.660 --> 01:06:17.220
I'm gonna see what
happens with other people"

01:06:17.220 --> 01:06:18.770
or "I'm not doing that," again,

01:06:20.850 --> 01:06:22.430
whether you think that
vaccines are safe or not,

01:06:22.430 --> 01:06:23.580
like if you think that they're not safe,

01:06:23.580 --> 01:06:26.630
you're still putting your
own safety and security

01:06:26.630 --> 01:06:30.200
above the collective, because
you know that without it,

01:06:30.200 --> 01:06:32.130
I'm just saying that it's a selfish act.

01:06:32.130 --> 01:06:33.610
- And I'll even say, I mean,

01:06:33.610 --> 01:06:36.840
we're talking about having the spirit

01:06:36.840 --> 01:06:38.650
and then looking at those that don't,

01:06:38.650 --> 01:06:41.060
and what that does deep down.

01:06:41.060 --> 01:06:42.300
I've never thought about this,

01:06:42.300 --> 01:06:45.313
but I do think it does have an impact.

01:06:47.143 --> 01:06:50.600
It alters the equation
in how you love somebody.

01:06:50.600 --> 01:06:53.680
I mean, or it's something
that you have to navigate,

01:06:53.680 --> 01:06:57.713
you know, it just, you know,
if you really think about it,

01:06:57.713 --> 01:07:01.913
how it impacts the nature
of loving somebody,

01:07:03.600 --> 01:07:05.810
and you might say, "well, yeah, I want

01:07:05.810 --> 01:07:10.060
I want them to be in dwelt
with the Holy spirit as well.

01:07:10.060 --> 01:07:11.960
And I'm motivated by that,"

01:07:11.960 --> 01:07:14.550
but there's, it's dicey, right?

01:07:14.550 --> 01:07:16.670
There's another side to
that psychologically.

01:07:16.670 --> 01:07:18.670
I'm just, I'm just raising the question.

01:07:18.670 --> 01:07:21.380
We, you know, we don't have
to, we shouldn't get into it,

01:07:21.380 --> 01:07:24.623
but I'm just observing that it,

01:07:27.870 --> 01:07:29.710
it's a complicating factor

01:07:32.880 --> 01:07:35.460
to someone who believes they've
been dwelt with the spirit

01:07:35.460 --> 01:07:39.840
to love somebody who's
not, like wholeheartedly.

01:07:39.840 --> 01:07:42.743
- And I think that the thing
that I'm seeing is that

01:07:42.743 --> 01:07:45.460
it's not just difficult
to fully love them.

01:07:45.460 --> 01:07:47.160
It's more difficult to trust them.

01:07:48.310 --> 01:07:50.190
I think that that's what
we're seeing in our country,

01:07:50.190 --> 01:07:52.790
is there is an inability to trust people

01:07:52.790 --> 01:07:53.990
who are outside of the church.

01:07:53.990 --> 01:07:57.740
That's why it's been
fascinating to me that

01:07:57.740 --> 01:08:01.460
when you talk to an evangelical
Christian in light of 2020,

01:08:01.460 --> 01:08:03.240
they think that the biggest problem

01:08:03.240 --> 01:08:05.520
is that they are being persecuted.

01:08:05.520 --> 01:08:07.270
They think that the biggest thing is that

01:08:07.270 --> 01:08:09.720
there is this socialist Marxist movement

01:08:09.720 --> 01:08:11.370
that is out to get them,

01:08:11.370 --> 01:08:13.720
to eradicate Christianity from the earth.

01:08:13.720 --> 01:08:16.640
to literally like if you
were to ask the average

01:08:18.370 --> 01:08:20.730
evangelical Christian,
like what are the chances

01:08:20.730 --> 01:08:25.328
that being a Christian will
be illegal in 25 years?

01:08:25.328 --> 01:08:28.920
I think you would get some, I
think they'd say pretty high.

01:08:28.920 --> 01:08:30.040
I remember thinking like that.

01:08:30.040 --> 01:08:31.970
I thought that it was all
headed towards this place

01:08:31.970 --> 01:08:34.870
where Christianity was gonna
become more and more illegal.

01:08:35.773 --> 01:08:38.190
- There are biblical interpretations

01:08:38.190 --> 01:08:40.760
that take you to that place.

01:08:40.760 --> 01:08:42.640
- Right, and we all,
everybody who's living

01:08:42.640 --> 01:08:44.893
always thinks that they're
in the end times, of course,

01:08:45.810 --> 01:08:47.670
but really what I'm getting at is

01:08:47.670 --> 01:08:52.290
this dualistic individualistic
approach to faith

01:08:52.290 --> 01:08:53.940
is something that I have had my fill of.

01:08:53.940 --> 01:08:56.510
I've did a lot of that, right?

01:08:56.510 --> 01:08:58.830
And so naturally as I kind of moved,

01:08:58.830 --> 01:09:00.660
continued to try to lean into the mystery

01:09:00.660 --> 01:09:04.670
and be a spiritual person and
move towards spiritual things,

01:09:04.670 --> 01:09:07.660
I'm naturally a lot more
attracted to more inclusive

01:09:07.660 --> 01:09:10.760
ways of thinking that focus on
interconnectedness of humans

01:09:10.760 --> 01:09:13.070
with each other and with the earth,

01:09:13.070 --> 01:09:15.230
you know, with nature in general.

01:09:15.230 --> 01:09:17.990
So that's what I tend
to explore right now.

01:09:17.990 --> 01:09:20.650
And I know that sounds sort of

01:09:20.650 --> 01:09:22.403
ambiguous, nebulous, whatever.

01:09:23.380 --> 01:09:25.080
Ultimately what I'm saying is that

01:09:26.100 --> 01:09:29.340
faith systems that are
built on an us versus them,

01:09:29.340 --> 01:09:32.300
or it's like, I have
something and you need it,

01:09:32.300 --> 01:09:35.180
versus we've all got something

01:09:35.180 --> 01:09:37.280
and we've all got a connection
to not only each other

01:09:37.280 --> 01:09:39.023
but to something beyond us.

01:09:41.070 --> 01:09:44.590
I feel like not only, I'm
personally more inspired

01:09:44.590 --> 01:09:46.580
and attracted to that right now,

01:09:46.580 --> 01:09:48.790
but I also think it's better for the world

01:09:48.790 --> 01:09:51.740
and where we're at as a species.

01:09:51.740 --> 01:09:54.320
Now, when I told my story,

01:09:54.320 --> 01:09:57.358
I used the infamous analogy of saying that

01:09:57.358 --> 01:10:00.368
Christianity was a ship in the ocean

01:10:00.368 --> 01:10:04.750
and that I jumped off of the
ship into a sea of uncertainty

01:10:04.750 --> 01:10:07.910
and I dragged my wife
and my children with me.

01:10:07.910 --> 01:10:09.680
I talked a little bit about
this on the Christmas episode,

01:10:09.680 --> 01:10:12.630
about how I regretted that
analogy for a number of reasons,

01:10:14.320 --> 01:10:16.390
chiefly is what it made it seem like

01:10:16.390 --> 01:10:17.920
I was saying about my wife.

01:10:17.920 --> 01:10:20.260
I was making it seem
like my wife and my kids,

01:10:20.260 --> 01:10:22.700
but especially my wife had no agency,

01:10:22.700 --> 01:10:25.240
that well, whatever the guy decides,

01:10:25.240 --> 01:10:26.543
the girl has to go along with it.

01:10:26.543 --> 01:10:31.010
It's like, and I regret even implying that

01:10:31.010 --> 01:10:36.010
because my wife Jessie has, (chuckles)

01:10:37.960 --> 01:10:39.400
just go to her Twitter account

01:10:39.400 --> 01:10:43.963
if you're interested in seeing
her exercise her agency.

01:10:43.963 --> 01:10:45.940
(laughing)

01:10:45.940 --> 01:10:50.493
LosFamgeles, L-O-S-F-A-M-Geles.

01:10:51.810 --> 01:10:53.760
- I hope she adds that to her Twitter bio,

01:10:53.760 --> 01:10:56.030
exercising my agency.

01:10:56.030 --> 01:10:58.460
- Meaning that Jessie has her own story.

01:10:58.460 --> 01:11:00.550
I think she'll tell it at some point.

01:11:00.550 --> 01:11:03.970
And Jessie is, she is,

01:11:03.970 --> 01:11:08.110
to characterize what she
went through is some sort of

01:11:08.110 --> 01:11:12.210
me leading our family down a
road to question Christianity

01:11:12.210 --> 01:11:13.973
and her coming along for ride is a,

01:11:16.060 --> 01:11:20.600
is a very large misrepresentation
of what actually happened.

01:11:20.600 --> 01:11:22.153
I may have been the first person to ask

01:11:22.153 --> 01:11:23.680
some of the tougher questions,

01:11:23.680 --> 01:11:27.940
but she has had her own
distinct journey in many ways

01:11:27.940 --> 01:11:32.080
much deeper and more
significant than my own.

01:11:32.080 --> 01:11:34.010
And she's challenged me in a lot of ways

01:11:34.010 --> 01:11:36.000
and also been really instrumental

01:11:36.000 --> 01:11:37.810
in my own personal spiritual growth.

01:11:37.810 --> 01:11:42.810
So it isn't about me leading
my family into despair,

01:11:44.930 --> 01:11:48.780
but I also regret the analogy a little bit

01:11:48.780 --> 01:11:53.740
because I didn't fully
explain what, you know,

01:11:53.740 --> 01:11:56.980
having reflected on it, the
point that I was trying to make

01:11:56.980 --> 01:12:00.553
was also that the ship on
the ocean of uncertainty

01:12:00.553 --> 01:12:02.890
that seemed to have a purpose
and seemed to be like,

01:12:02.890 --> 01:12:04.820
there's a bunch of people on that ship.

01:12:04.820 --> 01:12:07.740
I believe that was an illusion
of certainty and security.

01:12:07.740 --> 01:12:10.030
I don't believe that the ship existed

01:12:10.030 --> 01:12:11.480
or that it was just a collective of people

01:12:11.480 --> 01:12:13.520
who decided to gather
together, and I don't believe

01:12:13.520 --> 01:12:15.320
that it actually
represented the foundation

01:12:15.320 --> 01:12:17.280
that I thought that it did.

01:12:17.280 --> 01:12:18.413
That's why I jumped.

01:12:21.035 --> 01:12:22.700
But I was recently listening, you know,

01:12:22.700 --> 01:12:26.393
one of my favorite
philosophers is Alan Watts.

01:12:27.880 --> 01:12:32.880
And, you know, he's like
a '60s-based writer,

01:12:34.940 --> 01:12:38.240
professor, I don't even know
really what his background was,

01:12:38.240 --> 01:12:39.960
but he's just, he's just a trippy dude

01:12:39.960 --> 01:12:42.420
who says a lot of things
that sort of expand your mind

01:12:42.420 --> 01:12:43.980
and make you think
about things differently

01:12:43.980 --> 01:12:47.000
and I enjoy listening to him

01:12:47.000 --> 01:12:50.530
and just, again, just like having my mind

01:12:50.530 --> 01:12:52.310
kind of like challenged and expanded

01:12:52.310 --> 01:12:54.410
because of the way that he sees the world.

01:12:55.890 --> 01:12:57.520
And I wouldn't say that it's necessarily

01:12:57.520 --> 01:12:59.360
a spiritual practice to
listen to Alan Watts.

01:12:59.360 --> 01:13:02.360
It is somewhat, but for
me, it's kind of just fun.

01:13:02.360 --> 01:13:04.680
- It's certainly a philosophical practice.

01:13:04.680 --> 01:13:08.130
- Yeah, right, and it is
an expansive way to think.

01:13:08.130 --> 01:13:10.700
And he does talk a lot about
things that I relate to

01:13:10.700 --> 01:13:13.320
as I consider where I come from

01:13:13.320 --> 01:13:15.720
and where I might be going,
spiritually speaking.

01:13:15.720 --> 01:13:20.720
- He'll filter the Western point of view

01:13:20.900 --> 01:13:23.780
through Taoism, Buddhism,

01:13:23.780 --> 01:13:26.456
he'll kind of give you a
primer on those things.

01:13:26.456 --> 01:13:27.730
- And that's it, and so he
was talking specifically

01:13:27.730 --> 01:13:30.010
about this and he used the same analogy

01:13:30.010 --> 01:13:32.287
of jumping off of a ship.

01:13:32.287 --> 01:13:33.830
Or he may have said a rock,

01:13:33.830 --> 01:13:36.480
but what he was saying is
that if you read the Bible,

01:13:38.023 --> 01:13:41.393
the analogy is used for Jesus

01:13:41.393 --> 01:13:43.860
and just the faith in Christianity,

01:13:43.860 --> 01:13:46.570
it's a rock, it's a foundation,

01:13:46.570 --> 01:13:51.360
it's a steady, stable thing
that you need to be on.

01:13:51.360 --> 01:13:52.450
You need to be rooted.

01:13:52.450 --> 01:13:54.850
You need to, you know,
you need to be planted

01:13:54.850 --> 01:13:58.500
in a sea of uncertainty so
that you can kind of withstand

01:13:58.500 --> 01:14:00.785
all the things that
life would bring at you.

01:14:00.785 --> 01:14:03.690
And he was saying in
contrast to that, you know,

01:14:03.690 --> 01:14:08.690
Buddhism kind of represents
jumping off of that foundation

01:14:09.890 --> 01:14:13.665
into a sea of nothingness.

01:14:13.665 --> 01:14:16.640
And he says the only way to survive

01:14:16.640 --> 01:14:20.230
in the sea of nothingness is to swim.

01:14:20.230 --> 01:14:22.573
And the only way to swim is to relax.

01:14:23.710 --> 01:14:26.600
You know, if you're drowning in the ocean,

01:14:26.600 --> 01:14:29.270
a lot of people say
that if you can't swim,

01:14:29.270 --> 01:14:32.000
don't just go crazy, you'll
sink if you just start trying

01:14:32.000 --> 01:14:35.630
to tread water, the most humans float,

01:14:35.630 --> 01:14:38.760
if you just relax and float,

01:14:38.760 --> 01:14:40.670
your head will stay above water.

01:14:40.670 --> 01:14:43.860
And I was like, man, this
is a, this is a kind of,

01:14:43.860 --> 01:14:47.760
this is a great way to sort of reinterpret

01:14:47.760 --> 01:14:51.533
and reapply my analogy because

01:14:51.533 --> 01:14:55.240
I don't feel like I'm
in a sea of uncertainty.

01:14:55.240 --> 01:14:58.944
I feel like I'm free. Right?

01:14:58.944 --> 01:15:02.420
I would mostly describe
where I'm at right now

01:15:02.420 --> 01:15:05.110
as there is a sense of
lightness and a freedom,

01:15:05.110 --> 01:15:07.930
Ironically, that is the
way many Christians,

01:15:07.930 --> 01:15:10.500
especially people who have, you know,

01:15:10.500 --> 01:15:11.670
we didn't have the privilege of having

01:15:11.670 --> 01:15:13.790
like a hardcore testimony
like some people,

01:15:13.790 --> 01:15:14.710
you know, every once in a while

01:15:14.710 --> 01:15:17.470
when people did testimony
time, you got like the guy

01:15:17.470 --> 01:15:19.907
who was like, I was in
The Church of Satan,

01:15:19.907 --> 01:15:22.269
and I was a biker and I killed a boy.

01:15:22.269 --> 01:15:23.369
(laughing)
- Oh gosh.

01:15:24.960 --> 01:15:27.140
- And then I found Jesus
and now there's a sense

01:15:27.140 --> 01:15:29.220
that a burden has been lifted off of me.

01:15:29.220 --> 01:15:31.910
And when you grow up in the
church, you don't get that.

01:15:31.910 --> 01:15:34.080
You don't have that crazy, I was crazy

01:15:34.080 --> 01:15:36.663
and did all this stuff,
and now I got Jesus.

01:15:37.900 --> 01:15:39.970
But the way a lot of
people describe the moment

01:15:39.970 --> 01:15:41.360
of coming to faith there's a sense that

01:15:41.360 --> 01:15:43.247
a burden has been lifted off of them

01:15:43.247 --> 01:15:46.150
and there is a lightness
and they feel free.

01:15:46.150 --> 01:15:47.540
I'm not arguing with that,

01:15:47.540 --> 01:15:50.560
especially if you've got
a background where you,

01:15:50.560 --> 01:15:52.170
you know, you were all about yourself

01:15:52.170 --> 01:15:54.800
and then you gave yourself
to something else.

01:15:54.800 --> 01:15:59.400
But I just find that
to be ironic that the,

01:15:59.400 --> 01:16:04.400
for me, the leaving and also
being vocal about my leaving

01:16:04.875 --> 01:16:08.763
has been the sense of freedom,

01:16:10.470 --> 01:16:13.290
the sense of lightness, the
sense of not being burdened,

01:16:13.290 --> 01:16:15.500
the sense of being open,

01:16:15.500 --> 01:16:17.680
and the sense of not feeling threatened.

01:16:17.680 --> 01:16:20.690
I think one of the things that
was so true of my experience

01:16:20.690 --> 01:16:24.966
as a Christian was that
everything was a threat.

01:16:24.966 --> 01:16:26.423
Everything was a threat.

01:16:27.740 --> 01:16:31.173
You think this? Well, that might be scary.

01:16:33.070 --> 01:16:34.690
You might be out to get me.

01:16:34.690 --> 01:16:36.260
You might be bad for me.

01:16:36.260 --> 01:16:37.740
- I know where that leads.

01:16:37.740 --> 01:16:38.573
- Yeah.

01:16:38.573 --> 01:16:39.406
- Kind of a thing.

01:16:39.406 --> 01:16:41.853
- And what I have found
is that, you know what,

01:16:43.730 --> 01:16:46.110
when I jumped out of
the ship of Christianity

01:16:46.110 --> 01:16:48.450
into the sea of nothingness, (chuckles)

01:16:48.450 --> 01:16:50.860
which still may be a bad analogy,

01:16:50.860 --> 01:16:55.740
it's not like my moral
compass was on the ship.

01:16:55.740 --> 01:16:58.790
I think a lot of people live
with the understanding that,

01:16:58.790 --> 01:17:00.180
well, no the compass on the ship.

01:17:00.180 --> 01:17:02.340
If you leave the ship, you
don't have the compass anymore.

01:17:02.340 --> 01:17:04.310
It's like, no, you, you still, you got it.

01:17:04.310 --> 01:17:08.240
It's actually, unless you're
just biologically predisposed

01:17:08.240 --> 01:17:11.319
to be a horrible person,

01:17:11.319 --> 01:17:15.570
most people have a pretty
intact moral compass.

01:17:15.570 --> 01:17:17.830
And that sense of truth
and justice that your kids

01:17:17.830 --> 01:17:21.370
naturally have, that your
kid, that Lando naturally has.

01:17:21.370 --> 01:17:24.640
And it's been interesting
to navigate the world

01:17:24.640 --> 01:17:26.120
in a way that it doesn't mean that

01:17:26.120 --> 01:17:28.180
my guard is completely down.

01:17:28.180 --> 01:17:30.610
I still, in fact, one of the things

01:17:30.610 --> 01:17:32.310
that I've gotten better at recognizing,

01:17:32.310 --> 01:17:36.873
especially through therapy,
is malignant narcissism.

01:17:37.851 --> 01:17:41.920
As someone who is at least
somewhat narcissistic myself,

01:17:41.920 --> 01:17:44.173
you know it when you see it,

01:17:47.340 --> 01:17:50.350
but malignant narcissism and the idea that

01:17:50.350 --> 01:17:53.390
somebody is ultimately about
themselves above all things

01:17:53.390 --> 01:17:57.150
is something that any
organization, any system

01:17:57.150 --> 01:18:00.200
is a breeding ground for
narcissists to rise to the top.

01:18:00.200 --> 01:18:01.800
And the church has no exception.

01:18:03.680 --> 01:18:06.910
The story of the mega church
pastor who falls from grace,

01:18:06.910 --> 01:18:10.530
which is just, you know,
every month or every year

01:18:10.530 --> 01:18:12.920
there's gonna be another
big story like that.

01:18:12.920 --> 01:18:15.900
It's a case of malignant narcissism

01:18:15.900 --> 01:18:19.257
being rewarded in the church.

01:18:19.257 --> 01:18:23.980
And I think that I've actually
gotten better at recognizing

01:18:23.980 --> 01:18:26.000
people who are out for themselves

01:18:26.000 --> 01:18:29.360
and people who actually
are a threat to me,

01:18:29.360 --> 01:18:31.470
my family, and my wellbeing.

01:18:31.470 --> 01:18:33.100
And then to realize
that a lot of the people

01:18:33.100 --> 01:18:35.010
that I thought were threats,
and a lot of the ideas

01:18:35.010 --> 01:18:37.263
that I thought were threatening are not.

01:18:38.510 --> 01:18:40.400
And that is, I don't know, man,

01:18:40.400 --> 01:18:45.110
I'm just having a lot more fun
as a human than I did before.

01:18:45.110 --> 01:18:46.900
And that doesn't mean that I'm--

01:18:46.900 --> 01:18:47.733
- Dirty fun?

01:18:47.733 --> 01:18:50.120
- Doing a bunch of wild things that would,

01:18:50.120 --> 01:18:52.010
that would blow your mind if I told you

01:18:52.010 --> 01:18:54.420
and you know, compromise who I am

01:18:54.420 --> 01:18:59.060
and you know, end my
marriage, no, far from it.

01:18:59.060 --> 01:19:02.830
And, but I will say that,

01:19:02.830 --> 01:19:05.937
so the idea that Christianity
is kind of about, you know,

01:19:05.937 --> 01:19:08.010
it's foundational, it's a rock,

01:19:08.010 --> 01:19:10.660
you kind of got it figured
out and you go from there,

01:19:11.630 --> 01:19:14.930
moving away from that
into a more open place

01:19:14.930 --> 01:19:17.611
that doesn't have a system or a structure,

01:19:17.611 --> 01:19:20.510
I don't know, this may be a season,

01:19:20.510 --> 01:19:22.770
because I also don't
think necessarily that

01:19:24.200 --> 01:19:28.380
a systemless way of going
about things spiritually

01:19:28.380 --> 01:19:31.300
is sustainable, which kind of
gets back to what I just said

01:19:31.300 --> 01:19:34.240
which is this whole
religiously unaffiliated.

01:19:34.240 --> 01:19:37.660
So I don't know what this
means for me personally.

01:19:37.660 --> 01:19:40.920
Right now, it means that I'm reading

01:19:40.920 --> 01:19:41.753
a lot of different things,

01:19:41.753 --> 01:19:44.285
listening to a lot of different things,

01:19:44.285 --> 01:19:46.600
and having what I would describe as

01:19:46.600 --> 01:19:48.573
significant spiritual experiences.

01:19:49.560 --> 01:19:51.470
And again, a lot of these things

01:19:51.470 --> 01:19:53.160
are not things I want to
talk about explicitly.

01:19:53.160 --> 01:19:54.310
They're kind of ineffable

01:19:54.310 --> 01:19:56.270
and I feel like you can take
a little bit of the magic

01:19:56.270 --> 01:19:58.110
away from them when you
start trying to explain them

01:19:58.110 --> 01:19:59.623
and put them into human words.

01:20:01.160 --> 01:20:05.730
But I do think that spirituality

01:20:05.730 --> 01:20:07.650
is best experienced in community.

01:20:07.650 --> 01:20:11.080
And I think the idea
that I'm just some guy

01:20:11.080 --> 01:20:14.600
who like listens to something
on my headphones by myself

01:20:14.600 --> 01:20:16.080
and that's my spiritual experience

01:20:16.080 --> 01:20:19.330
is not the end all be all.

01:20:19.330 --> 01:20:20.920
I think one of the things that the church

01:20:20.920 --> 01:20:23.220
does very very well is community.

01:20:23.220 --> 01:20:25.380
And I think that we need community.

01:20:25.380 --> 01:20:27.050
We need connection.

01:20:27.050 --> 01:20:30.130
And I think that any true
religion, any true faith,

01:20:30.130 --> 01:20:33.215
any true spirituality is going to be about

01:20:33.215 --> 01:20:35.720
benevolent connection between people.

01:20:35.720 --> 01:20:37.020
It's not gonna be about hierarchy.

01:20:37.020 --> 01:20:39.680
It's not gonna be about
one narcissistic dude

01:20:39.680 --> 01:20:41.220
making all the rules in charge,

01:20:41.220 --> 01:20:43.930
which happens in systems a lot of times,

01:20:43.930 --> 01:20:48.760
but true benevolence in grace with,

01:20:48.760 --> 01:20:50.980
from one person to another.

01:20:50.980 --> 01:20:53.240
And like my wife and I experience that

01:20:53.240 --> 01:20:56.260
you know, as we have spiritual
experiences together.

01:20:56.260 --> 01:21:00.130
But again, I think that ultimately,

01:21:00.130 --> 01:21:01.550
and I don't know when this will happen,

01:21:01.550 --> 01:21:04.880
but I think that it's got to happen

01:21:04.880 --> 01:21:06.350
in the context of community.

01:21:06.350 --> 01:21:09.530
But as soon as you apply
labels and systems to it,

01:21:09.530 --> 01:21:11.593
humans get involved and
end up doing the things

01:21:11.593 --> 01:21:14.060
that humans do, which is
they establish the hierarchy.

01:21:14.060 --> 01:21:16.434
They reinforce the patriarchy,

01:21:16.434 --> 01:21:18.413
the narcissists rise to the top.

01:21:19.774 --> 01:21:22.260
So there's some interesting
conversations going on

01:21:22.260 --> 01:21:23.600
and have been going on for years

01:21:23.600 --> 01:21:25.860
about what does this mean for the church?

01:21:25.860 --> 01:21:30.860
Like if the church, you know
really wakes up from its,

01:21:31.117 --> 01:21:34.780
from the nightmare, and
again, there's lots of people

01:21:34.780 --> 01:21:36.240
who would say "listen
guys, you're just still

01:21:36.240 --> 01:21:38.700
talking about the evangelical
church or Protestants

01:21:38.700 --> 01:21:41.530
or, you know, there's
actually a lot of people

01:21:41.530 --> 01:21:43.560
who call themselves
Christians who are already

01:21:43.560 --> 01:21:44.670
where you're talking about

01:21:44.670 --> 01:21:46.630
and come be a part of that community."

01:21:46.630 --> 01:21:48.860
I'm not saying that I
won't, I'm not saying that

01:21:48.860 --> 01:21:50.060
that's not gonna happen.

01:21:52.000 --> 01:21:55.130
So far, I haven't gotten to
a place where I feel like

01:21:55.130 --> 01:21:58.840
I could comfortably reenter
that kind of environment

01:21:58.840 --> 01:22:03.840
that is saturated in Christian
language, ideas, and imagery,

01:22:04.170 --> 01:22:06.390
and not just go back
to the individualistic,

01:22:06.390 --> 01:22:08.973
dualistic way of thinking about things.

01:22:08.973 --> 01:22:12.000
So maybe there's a community out there

01:22:12.000 --> 01:22:14.010
where you can kind of
experience the openness

01:22:14.010 --> 01:22:16.300
and the expansiveness
and it's about connection

01:22:16.300 --> 01:22:19.200
with each other and something
beyond your collective self,

01:22:20.334 --> 01:22:22.077
'cause I'm not interested in saying,

01:22:22.077 --> 01:22:23.620
"Hey, I'm gonna go be a Buddhist.

01:22:23.620 --> 01:22:27.290
I'm gonna go and be a part of a tradition

01:22:27.290 --> 01:22:28.790
that I have no knowledge of."

01:22:28.790 --> 01:22:31.137
And you know what I'm saying?

01:22:31.137 --> 01:22:33.600
Like, I don't think the answer
is to go find another system

01:22:33.600 --> 01:22:35.710
or subscribe to some new faith

01:22:35.710 --> 01:22:38.800
and become like a
inductee into some other,

01:22:38.800 --> 01:22:39.633
you know what I'm saying?

01:22:39.633 --> 01:22:42.250
Like that doesn't feel right right now.

01:22:42.250 --> 01:22:46.960
So I don't know, a lot
of it is up in the air,

01:22:46.960 --> 01:22:51.100
but I don't feel an emptiness right now

01:22:51.100 --> 01:22:54.160
and I don't feel like this great desire

01:22:54.160 --> 01:22:58.726
to immediately define what
it is that I'm experiencing,

01:22:58.726 --> 01:23:02.030
to try to invite other people into it.

01:23:02.030 --> 01:23:05.110
I'm not, I'm trying not to
be evangelistic about it.

01:23:05.110 --> 01:23:06.800
I tend to be evangelistic in general,

01:23:06.800 --> 01:23:09.800
meaning that I try to share and persuade.

01:23:09.800 --> 01:23:12.140
And I'm trying not to do that
with what I'm experiencing

01:23:12.140 --> 01:23:14.320
and probably the fact that I don't,

01:23:14.320 --> 01:23:16.460
you can't even really put your finger

01:23:16.460 --> 01:23:17.610
on what I'm saying about it

01:23:17.610 --> 01:23:21.397
was probably helpful
to that end, but yeah,

01:23:21.397 --> 01:23:23.540
that's kinda where I'm at right now,

01:23:23.540 --> 01:23:25.230
is there's just this anticipation

01:23:26.635 --> 01:23:30.470
that we're all moving in a direction.

01:23:30.470 --> 01:23:34.503
And I want to, you know, I want
to be where the magic's at.

01:23:35.460 --> 01:23:38.163
- Oh, okay. That's a good
place to end with, magic.

